# SIT315 Module 1 – Interrupt-Based Sense–Think–Act System

## Project Overview

This project demonstrates an interrupt-based embedded system developed using an Arduino Uno in the Wokwi simulator. The system follows the Sense–Think–Act model and uses multiple interrupt techniques to respond to real-time events. The project was completed as part of the SIT315 Module 1 assessment.

## Features

- External Interrupt using `attachInterrupt()` on pin D2
- Pin Change Interrupts (PCI) on pins D8 and D9
- Timer1 Interrupt generating a heartbeat every second
- Sense–Think–Act decision-making model
- Alarm LED control based on sensor conditions
- Real-time Serial Monitor output
- Event-driven programming using Interrupt Service Routines (ISRs)

## Hardware / Components

- Arduino Uno
- 3 Push Buttons
  - Door Sensor (D2)
  - Window Sensor (D8)
  - Emergency Sensor (D9)
- Red LED
- 220Ω Resistor
- Wokwi Arduino Simulator

## System Logic

### Sense
The Arduino monitors three push buttons using interrupt-based programming. The door sensor uses an external interrupt, while the window and emergency sensors use pin change interrupts. Timer1 generates a periodic interrupt every second.

### Think
The program checks the current sensor states and decides whether the system is safe or if the alarm should be activated.

### Act
The alarm LED turns ON during emergency conditions or when required by the system logic. The Serial Monitor displays the current system status and interrupt events.

## Project Files

- `sketch.ino` – Main Arduino program
- `diagram.json` – Wokwi circuit diagram
- `Task M1QP - Comprehensive Interrupt-Based Sense-Think-Act System.pdf` – Project report

## Testing

The following functions were successfully tested:

- Door sensor external interrupt
- Window pin change interrupt
- Emergency pin change interrupt
- Timer1 heartbeat interrupt
- Alarm LED operation
- Serial Monitor output

## Simulation

The project was developed and tested using the **Wokwi Arduino Simulator**.

